 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcXoJRjpglA/",
 "title": "How to Ask Specific Questions to Get Real Answers",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "Asking generic questions leads to predictable, standard answers because candidates rely on rehearsed scripts. Reframing interview prompts to ask about specific past experiences forces candidates to retrieve actual memories and reveal genuine insights.",
 "key_points": [
  "Generic questions invite generic answers; specific questions prompt unique, authentic responses.",
  "Instead of asking 'What are your strengths?', ask 'What do your colleagues keep coming to you for help on?'",
  "Instead of asking 'How do you usually handle conflict?', ask 'Tell me about the last time that you disagreed with a peer.'",
  "Instead of asking 'Describe your ideal work environment', frame it around past experience: 'What were some of your favorite things about your favorite team in your career experience?'",
  "Changing the angle of a question forces candidates to talk about real situations instead of listing generic traits."
 ],
 "content": "Post Text:\nStandard prompts invite standard answers. Try changing the angle of the question so they have to reach for a real memory instead of a script.\n\nSome examples:\n\n• Instead of “What are your strengths?”\n“What do people on your team constantly tap you on the shoulder for?”\n\n• Instead of “How do you usually handle conflict?”\n“Tell me about the last time you disagreed with someone at work. What happened?”\n\n• Instead of “Describe your ideal work environment.”\n“Think about the best team you’ve ever been on. What made that team feel different?”\n\nEach tweak forces the candidate to talk about specific situations, not generic traits.\n\nOnce you hear real stories, separating great from average gets a lot easier.\n\nMake a note of this post.\n\nTranscript:\nActually, let's do this one. Do you have pen and paper near you? So I'm going to give you 30 seconds. Go ahead and just draw me a sunset. Okay, cool. So I want you all to share, show it in the screen. What does your sunset look like? Okay, cool. So drop it, and I want you to give me a thumbs up if your sunset has a horizon line. Tell me if it has a sun with sun rays. Tell me if it has a tree. Usually when you're given this exercise, those are the attributes that we tend to see. Now I want you to do it again, and this time I want you to draw the last sunset that you remember. So go ahead and show that to the rest of the class. Did you notice how different these are from each other? Generic questions will give us generic answers. Specific questions will give us specific answers. So for example, instead of telling me like your strengths, what do your colleagues keep coming to you for help on? That's a much more powerful question that you can ask. Tell me about the last time that you disagreed with a peer, as opposed to like, how do you usually handle conflict? Sometimes we ask, can you tell me a little bit about your ideal work environment? Be like, okay, I see in your resume that you've been part of five different teams. So I'm imagining that you saw some good things and some bad things as part of that. What were some of your favorite things about your favorite team in your career experience? Now, instead of remembering generic answers about work environment, I get to think about my favorite team three years ago where we were all like doing our jobs, collaborating with each other, and we used to hang out after every Friday. Whatever, right? We should be able to stretch those muscles and ask a question in a different way so that we can get truer answers.\n\nOn-Screen Text:\n- Why All Candidates Sound The Same\n- Draw a picture of a sunset 🌅\n- Tip: Generic questions will give us generic answers. Specific questions will give us specific answers.\n- Generic Questions: What are your strenghts? | Specific Questions: What do your colleagues keep coming to you for help on?\n- Generic Questions: How do you usually handle conflict? | Specific Questions: Tell me about the las time that you disagreed with a peer\n- Generic Questions: Can you tell me a little about your ideal work enviroment? | Specific Questions: What were some of your favorite things about your favorite team in your career experience?",
 "status": "done",
